[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Accessibility (a11y) and Dynamic Theming in Android (Kotlin)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern Android development requires applications to be fully inclusive and adaptable to user preferences. Combining accessibility (a11y) with dynamic theming (Material You) ensures your app is usable by everyone, regardless of visual impairment or UI customization preferences."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Implementing Dynamic Theming with Material 3"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Material 3 introduces dynamic color, allowing your app to adopt the user\'s wallpaper and system accent colors. To maintain accessibility, these dynamically generated palettes are automatically tuned for correct contrast ratios."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@Composable
fun AppTheme(
    darkTheme: Boolean = isSystemInDarkTheme(),
    dynamicColor: Boolean = true,
    content: @Composable () -> Unit
) {
    val context = LocalContext.current
    val colorScheme = when {
        dynamicColor && Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= Build.VERSION_CODES.S -> {
            if (darkTheme) dynamicDarkColorScheme(context) else dynamicLightColorScheme(context)
        }
        darkTheme -> darkColorScheme()
        else -> lightColorScheme()
    }

    MaterialTheme(
        colorScheme = colorScheme,
        typography = AppTypography,
        content = content
    )
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Ensuring Contrast Compliance for a11y"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"When relying on dynamic theming, text and interactive elements must meet WCAG contrast guidelines (at least 4.5:1 for normal text). Material 3 color roles (like onPrimary, primaryContainer) are specifically engineered to guarantee these ratios."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use semantic color tokens from MaterialTheme.colorScheme instead of hardcoded hex values."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Test your app using the Accessibility Scanner app to catch low-contrast text automatically."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Adding Content Descriptions and Semantics in Jetpack Compose"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Screen readers like TalkBack rely on semantic properties to describe UI components. Decorative images should be hidden from accessibility services, while interactive elements must have clear labels."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"@Composable
fun AccessibleIconButton(
    iconRes: Int,
    contentDescription: String,
    onClick: () -> Unit
) {
    IconButton(onClick = onClick) {
        Icon(
            painter = painterResource(id = iconRes),
            contentDescription = contentDescription
        )
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Handling Custom Touch Targets"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Accessibility guidelines require interactive elements to have a minimum touch target size of 48x48dp. You can enforce this in Compose using the modifier extension."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"@Composable
fun AccessibleButton(text: String, onClick: () -> Unit) {
    Box(
        modifier = Modifier
            .sizeIn(minWidth = 48.dp, minHeight = 48.dp)
            .clickable(onClick = onClick),
        contentAlignment = Alignment.Center
    ) {
        Text(text = text)
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining Material 3 dynamic color schemes with robust Jetpack Compose semantics and proper touch targets, you build an inclusive Android experience that satisfies both aesthetic trends and accessibility laws."}]
